# ============================================================================
#  Valquo — merge behaviour for the files that several agent lanes edit at once
#
#  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: five or six Claude Code lanes run in parallel and each
#  pushes a `worktree-*` branch that the land Action merges into `main`. Where
#  two lanes edit the same lines, the merge fails and the branch does not land.
#  Measured over 2026-08-04..06: HANDOFF_STATUS.md took 29 commits from many
#  lanes in three days, and EVERY conflict so far was resolved by hand with the
#  same answer -- keep both sides. `merge=union` is git doing that itself.
#
#  WHAT UNION DOES: on a conflicting hunk it keeps BOTH sides' lines instead of
#  writing conflict markers. It never fails. That is right for append-per-lane
#  files and wrong for anything where one side is meant to REPLACE the other --
#  see the "deliberately NOT union" list below, which is the important half.
# ============================================================================

# Append-per-lane. Every session prepends its own section; nothing is ever meant
# to overwrite another lane's write-up, so "keep both" is always the right call.
HANDOFF_STATUS.md   merge=union

# Append-only by design (its own first line says so): one row per pre-registered
# test, new rows added at the end. Two lanes appending different rows is the
# normal case and union handles it exactly.
#
# The failure mode is a duplicate id inflating the trial count, because
# valuation/edge/research_log.py:_parse counts every table row and does not
# dedupe. That direction is SELF-PENALISING -- a larger N raises sr0_benchmark
# and LOWERS the Deflated Sharpe, i.e. it makes the project's own bar harder,
# never easier. A union artefact here cannot flatter a result.
RESEARCH_LOG.md     merge=union

# Per-lane handoffs: one owner each, so they should not conflict at all. Union
# is set as a backstop for the case where two sessions share a lane name -- the
# cost of a duplicated paragraph is far below the cost of a branch not landing.
HANDOFF_*.md        merge=union

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT UNION -- do not "tidy" these by adding them above.
#
#   VALQUO_LEDGER.md  A keyed table with a one-row-per-audit-item contract (134
#                     items). Union's failure mode is TWO rows with the same id
#                     and no rule for which wins -- silently breaking the only
#                     guarantee the file makes, in a file whose whole purpose is
#                     to be the authoritative answer to "where do we stand".
#                     Its edits are also spread across 134 distinct rows, so an
#                     ordinary 3-way merge already resolves most of them; a
#                     conflict means two lanes touched the SAME row, which is
#                     exactly when someone should look. And it is regenerable:
#                     `python scripts/build_ledger.py` reconciles it and will
#                     not overwrite `src=human` rows, only report disagreement.
#
#   CLAUDE.md         Corrections are made IN PLACE ("this claim was wrong, here
#                     is the right number"). Union would keep the old and the new
#                     claim side by side in the project brief -- the one file
#                     every session reads as fact. A conflict is much cheaper.
#
#   RUN_RULES.md      Same reasoning, and it barely changes (1 commit in 3 days).
#
#   *.py, *.json      Never union code or data. Union produces files that are
#                     syntactically broken or silently wrong, and it would defeat
#                     the test gate's purpose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NOT SET, deliberately: `* text=auto`. It is the usual first line of a
# .gitattributes, but this repo has CRLF working copies and enabling it would
# renormalise line endings across the whole tree -- a diff touching nearly every
# file, which would conflict with every branch currently open. That is precisely
# the churn this file exists to reduce.

# PDFs are BINARY. Not a style preference -- a measured defect: this repo runs
# core.autocrlf=true and sets no binary rule, so git falls back to its NUL-byte
# heuristic. reportlab output contains no NUL bytes, so git classified
# VALQUO_LIVE_AUDIT.pdf as TEXT and converted LF->CRLF on checkout: the working
# copy carries 308 CRLF sequences and pypdf reports "incorrect startxref
# pointer" on it. The stored blob is clean; every Windows checkout corrupts it.
# Marking only *.pdf keeps this narrow -- it is NOT `* text=auto`, so nothing is
# renormalised and no open branch is disturbed. (Master audit #3, MA35.)
*.pdf               binary

# THE APPEND-ONLY RECORDS ARE BINARY, for the same measured reason as *.pdf above and with a
# sharper consequence. `append_only.append` rewrites the whole file through `csv.writer` with
# `newline=""`, and csv's default line terminator is CRLF -- so every row the fleet writer
# emits is CRLF, and the APPEND-ONLY BYTE-PREFIX GUARANTEE the record's tamper-evidence rests
# on only holds if the stored bytes are CRLF too.
#
# With no rule, `core.autocrlf=true` decides that per COMMITTER: the CI bot commits from Linux
# and its CRLF survives, while anything committed from a Windows lane is normalised to LF on
# the way in. Measured 2026-10-19: the live `f3_bear_puts.csv` blob holds 433 CRLF and 0 bare
# LF, and a fixture copied from it by a Windows lane came back with 0 CRLF and 79 bare LF --
# 45,648 bytes against the 45,727 it was copied from. A record stored LF can never satisfy
# `after.startswith(before)` against a writer that re-emits CRLF, so this is a correctness
# rule for the record, not a style preference.
#
# Narrow on purpose, exactly as the *.pdf rule is: these two paths only, so nothing else is
# renormalised and no open branch is disturbed.
data_export/fleet_records/*.csv     binary
tests/frozen_records/**             binary
